  "_biasAdherence_comment": "ADDED 2026-08-13 (Anoop, verbatim): 'I should follow the trade direction according to it. 70% to 80% should be in the same direction.' targetPct 75 is the midpoint of the range he named; warnPct 60 is where Jessi starts naming it rather than just reporting it. DIRECTION OF RECORD = the Daily Bias field from the pre-trade checklist (his call 2026-08-13); 4H and 1H are logged as CONTEXT and downgrade confidence when they disagree, they do not override or veto. This became computable only on 2026-08-13: before that the checklist record stored {date, score, tier} and threw the bias/h4/h1 away, so there was nothing to compare trades against. minTradesToJudge stops a 1-trade day reading as '0% adherence' - one counter-trend scratch is not a pattern. Set enabled:false to silence the whole feature.",
  "autoPostSessionReview": false,
  "breakEvenBandUsd": 100,
  "breakEvenReminderCount": 5,
  "_breakEven_comment": "ADDED 2026-08-25, in Anoop's own words and his own numbers: \"There are so many trades in a day that I take break even. Consider anything below 100$ and above -100$ as not a trade... After 5 break even trades, I want you to remind me.\" A trade landing inside +/-breakEvenBandUsd is flagged BREAK-EVEN and excluded from the real-trade count; at breakEvenReminderCount of them, mistake-patterns F4 fires. On 2026-08-25, 8 of his 11 trades landed inside the band - about $77.90 of commission paid for trades that returned nothing. DELIBERATELY ADVISORY: break-even trades STILL count toward tradesPerDay/contractsPerDay. Excluding them from a SAFETY cap would make churn - the exact thing F4 exists to flag - the loophole that bypasses the ceiling. It also does NOT enter the flags array: disc is computed as 4 minus the number of rule-break flags, so flagging BREAK-EVEN there would silently score a clean small trade as a rule break.",
